# Chapter 6, last part: the command lines of the Printer and the print and
# preview modes of the Editor, for developers.  Strata is started with the
# Document Manager; these are not a supported way to start it, so the
# release edition leaves them out (the last sections of the chapter, so that
# the numbers of the others do not change).
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
.h2 Command lines of the Printer and the Editor [print-cmdline]
.ix Command line, printing
.ix Printing from a script
.ix Printer, command line
.ix --no-error-log
.ix --no-banner
A script can make a PDF without the screen of the Editor by starting the
Printer, or the Editor in its print mode or its preview mode.  This section
is for the people who write such scripts, test the programs or work on them.
Start Strata itself with the Document Manager.  {x:appcmd-dev} lists every
option of every program.
.h3 The Printer [print-cmdline-printer]
.h4 What the Printer makes
.table 14 10 26
Result | Option | What it is
A PDF file | (none) | The default.  The pages are those of Final.
DVI file for Preview | {c:--dvi} | The pages that Preview shows.
The LaTeX source | {c:--tex-only} | Only the {c:.tex} file is written.  You can edit
 it and run LaTeX yourself.
.end


.h4 The options of the Printer
.ix -o, option
.ix --dvi
.ix --tex-only
.ix --draft
.ix --double-spaced
.ix --no-error-log
.ix --no-banner
Exactly one document name is wanted: the first word that does not begin with
a minus.  A second such word, an option that is not valid and an
option that needs a value and has none make the Printer write a usage line on
the standard error and end with status 2.  The table holds the options that a
script needs; the Printer has more, for the Editor to use, and
{x:appcmd-strprint-opt} lists all of them.
.table 13 47
Option | Meaning
FILE.DOC | The document to typeset.  The name is used as given.
{c:-o OUT} | The output file.  The default is the name of the document with its
 extension replaced by {c:.pdf} (or {c:.dvi}), in the current directory, not in the
 directory of the document.  With {c:--tex-only} a trailing {c:.pdf} or {c:.dvi} of OUT is
 replaced by {c:.tex}, or {c:.tex} is added.
{c:--dvi} | Make a DVI file instead of a PDF.  The pictures of the document are
 copied next to it; a file of that name that exists with other contents is not
 replaced and a message reports this.
{c:--tex-only} | Write the LaTeX source and stop.  Status 0 when it was written.
{c:--draft} | Draft print: the comments of the document are printed, the line
 spacing is single and the space between blocks is that of one line.  The
 pictures are printed as in Final.
{c:--double-spaced} | Double the line spacing of every block.
{c:--no-error-log} | Do not end the file with the Error Log.  The faults are
 still written on the standard error, and the status is still 4
 ({n:print-errlog}).
{c:--no-banner} | Do not put the banner of a failed print on the pages.  The
 marks of the faults stay ({n:print-failed}).  {c:--banner} puts it back (it is
 the default).
.end


.h4 Exit status of the Printer
.ix Exit status, Printer
When the Printer makes a file, characters that no font has are printed as
[U+XXXX] and named once each on the standard error.  A piece that LaTeX does
not accept (an equation, a picture) is printed in the long form of its marker,
so that a document always gives a file; the pieces are reported as errors
({n:print-errors}).  The exit status says how the run ended.
.table 8 52
Status | Meaning
0 | The file was written (or, with {c:--tex-only}, the {c:.tex} file).
1 | The document cannot be read, the typesetting failed or did not run, or the
 output cannot be written.  The message names the first TeX error, or shows
 that the typesetting did not run.
2 | A mistake in the command line (the usage line is shown).
4 | The file was made, and the document has errors or warnings
 ({n:print-errors}).  With errors the print has failed ({n:print-failed}):
 every page has the banner (unless {c:--no-banner} was given) and the marks,
 and the standard error shows "PRINT FAILED".  The file has the Error Log
 unless {c:--no-error-log} was given.  With warnings only the file is as good
 as one with status 0.  One line for each fault is written on the standard
 error, and a count.
.end


.h3 The print and preview modes of the Editor [print-cli]
.ix Editor, print mode
.ix --print
.ix --preview
.ix Print from a script
.ix Exit status, print mode
The Editor can print and preview without showing its editing screen.  A script
can use these two modes.  They are selected by an option of the
Editor, followed by the name of the document and the other options below.

.h4 The print mode
The option {c:--print} selects it.  It makes the PDF and sends it, as the
Print panel does, without opening the editor.  It is Final unless
{c:--draft} is given.
.table 15 45
Option | Meaning
{c:--print} | Selects the print mode.  The first word without a minus is the
 document.
{c:--printer QUEUE} | The queue to send the PDF to.  Without this option the PDF is
 made and not sent (the Printer of Setup is the default of the panels only).
{c:--copies N} | 1 to 99.  Default 1.
{c:--sides WORD} | The word one asks for one-sided, the word two for two-sided on
 the long edge.  Any other word is refused: "--sides is one or two".
{c:--sort WORD} | The word yes asks to collate, the word no not to.  Any other
 word is refused: "--sort is yes or no".
{c:-o OUT.pdf} | The PDF.  The default is the document's path with its extension
 replaced by {c:.pdf} ({c:-draft.pdf} with {c:--draft}).
{c:--from SECTION} | The first section to print.
{c:--to SECTION} | The last section to print.
{c:--from-page N} | The first page for {c:lp}.
{c:--to-page N} | The last page for {c:lp}.
{c:--no-title} | Leave out the title page.
{c:--double-spaced} | Double the line spacing.
{c:--black-and-white} | Print every color as a grey (Print in Black and White?).
{c:--recover} | Print the part of a damaged document that can be read; the error
 output says what was left out.  Without it a damaged file is refused.
{c:--index}, {c:--no-index} | Make the index at the end, or leave it out; the
 document says when neither is given (Index?).
{c:--list-figures}, {c:--no-list-figures} | Make the list of figures, or not
 (List of Figures?).
{c:--list-pictures}, {c:--no-list-pictures} | The same for the list of pictures.
{c:--list-tables}, {c:--no-list-tables} | The same for the list of tables.
{c:--contents LEVEL} | Put a table of contents first, listing the headlines of
 the levels 1 to LEVEL (1 to 99); 0 leaves it out.  The document says when the
 option is not given.
{c:--draft} | Make a Draft.
{c:--no-error-log} | Do not end the PDF with the Error Log (Error Log?  No).
{c:--no-banner} | Do not put the banner on the pages of a failed print.  The
 marks stay ({n:print-failed}).
{c:--error-log}, {c:--banner} | Put the Error Log and the banner back.  They are
 the default; the options undo {c:--no-error-log} and {c:--no-banner}.
{c:--errors FILE} | Write the faults of the document to FILE, in the form that
 the Editor reads, with the exit status of the run.  If FILE cannot be
 written the Editor reports this on the standard error.
{c:--var NAME VALUE}, {c:--special NAME VALUE} | Give a variable of the conditional
 text, or a special variable, to the print ({n:str-variables}).  A refused
 name or value ends the run before it starts, with status 2.
{c:-v}, {c:--verbose} | Tell the progress on the standard error: "Page 1 of about 2",
 "Sending to the printer".
.end
The same refusals as in the panel apply to the values ({n:print-messages}); a refusal ends the run before anything is started.  The
Editor prints one line, and the exit status says what happened, so that a
shell script can use it.
.table 8 52
Status | Meaning
0 | The PDF is made, and sent if {c:--printer} was given.  The line is written on
 the standard output: "Wrote FILE.pdf (12 pages)" or "Wrote FILE.pdf (12
 pages) and sent it to QUEUE (request id is ...)".
1 | Nothing was made: the Printer or {c:pdflatex} is missing, LaTeX failed, the
 document cannot be read.  The text is on the standard error.
2 | A wrong option or value (the same refusals as the panel).  Nothing was
 started.
3 | The PDF is made but not sent: {c:lp} is missing, {c:lp} failed, or the page range
 does not fit the PDF.  The PDF is kept; the text is on the standard error.
4 | Stopped.  (A signal ends the program as the signal would.)
5 | The PDF is made (and sent, if {c:--printer} was given), but the document has
 errors or warnings.  The line "Wrote ..." is written as for status 0 (with
 errors it begins "PRINT FAILED: ", and the pages have the banner and the
 marks, {n:print-failed}), and the faults follow on the standard error under
 "FILE: the document has errors or warnings:" (and the line "PRINT FAILED:
 the output was made, but it is inaccurate." after it, when there are
 errors), one numbered line for each with its place, and a count that
 begins "PRINT FAILED: " when there are errors.  A document with no faults keeps status 0.
.end
The print mode does what {x:print-final} and {x:print-sending} describe.

.h4 The preview mode
The option {c:--preview} followed by a document name opens no editing screen:
it previews the whole document, with the title page, as Preview, Document
does, and returns when the Viewer is closed.  The option {c:--no-error-log}
leaves the Error Log out of the pages, and {c:--no-banner} the banner of a
failed print ({n:print-failed}).  When the document has errors or
warnings the status is 5, and the faults are written on the standard error
when the terminal is given back ({n:print-errtext}).  It needs an interactive
terminal or the X11 version.  Any result other than "Preview done" and
"Preview cancelled" is shown in a box titled "Preview Document" and the
status is 1; a document that cannot be read gives status 1.
.endif
